From cfc01959b0b3ff73cb6e71c55fe4e959d38395c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 18:41:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] make the e2e suite runnable outside the machine it was
 written on
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

scripts/e2e-smoke.mjs hardcoded executablePath: "/opt/pw-browsers/chromium",
which is the path inside my sandbox. It ran there and nowhere else — the
Codespace run failed with "executable doesn't exist at /opt/pw-browsers/chromium"
immediately after a completely green build. My mistake, and the kind that only
shows up on someone else's machine.

- Playwright now resolves its own browser. PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_PATH overrides
  it for environments that ship a binary outside Playwright's cache.
- A failed launch prints the one command that fixes it
  (`npx playwright install chromium`) instead of a stack trace.
- CI gains an `e2e` job that installs only Chromium (`--with-deps chromium`,
  not all three browsers) and runs the suite against the production build.

Also: chunkSizeWarningLimit 900 -> 1400. @react-pdf/renderer is 1.29 MB and is
deliberately behind a dynamic import, so it never touches the entry chunk.
Warning about it on every build trains people to ignore the warning, which is
worse than not having one.

Verified both paths: with a valid browser the suite is 14 passed / 0 failed;
with a bad PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_PATH it exits 1 with the install hint.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01GJwENjMMPe6PSZBdrXkzBj
